Give each isolated-service instance its own process, so Chrome's renderers stop
evicting each other.

SYMPTOM
    With patch 0009 the fatal locale crash is gone and Chrome reaches its first-run
    screen, but web content never renders. The renderer starts and dies, forever:

        BProcessManager: initProcess: com.android.chrome:sandboxed_process0
        cr_SplitCompatApp:  processName=com.android.chrome:sandboxed_process0
        BProcessManager: App Died: com.android.chrome:sandboxed_process0
        cr_ChildProcessConn: onServiceDisconnected (crash or killed by oom): pid=12429

    docs/multiapp-vs-duplika-chrome-comparison.md already measured the difference against
    a competitor: renderer instances 0-19 in distinct processes there, "never created"
    here. Its section 10 named this exact gap and deferred it:

        "It does not give Chrome multiple concurrent renderers ... every instance of
         SandboxedProcessService0 collapses into the single :p1 slot ... matching that
         needs instance-aware slot allocation, which is a larger change and should be a
         separate decision."

    This is that change.

CAUSE
    Context.bindIsolatedService(service, flags, instanceName, ...) is one process per
    instanceName, all from a single <service> declaration. Chrome uses it for renderers.

    Bcore allocates the proxy slot from the process record:

        ActiveServices.createStubServiceIntent:
            ProxyManifest.getProxyService(processRecord.bpid)

    and looks the record up by processName alone:

        BProcessManagerService.startProcessLocked:
            app = bProcess.get(processName);

    Every renderer instance shares one declared processName, so they share one record,
    one bpid and one ProxyService$PN. Each new bind takes the slot from the previous
    process, which then dies -- the loop above.

    Patch 0008 is upstream of this. It cleared the instance name (args[6] = null) to stop
    AMS rejecting the bind, which was correct as far as it went: it let the first renderer
    start. But it also made every instance indistinguishable, which is what collapses them.

THE CHANGE
    Separate the map key from the guest-visible process name.

    1. ProcessRecord gains `processKey`, the key the record is stored under. Equal to
       processName for every ordinary process.

    2. BProcessManagerService.startProcessLocked gains a 6-argument overload taking
       processKey; the 5-argument form delegates with processKey = processName, so every
       existing caller is unchanged. get/put/remove use processKey, and onProcessDie
       removes record.processKey rather than record.processName -- otherwise death cleanup
       would leak the entry it created.

    3. ActiveServices.bindService derives processKey as processName + "@" + instanceName
       when the bind carries one, and passes processName through untouched. The guest still
       sees its real process name; only Bcore's internal map distinguishes instances.

    4. IActivityManagerProxy.bindServiceInstance stashes the instance name on the intent
       before clearing the argument. args[6] is still nulled for the host AMS call, so
       patch 0008's fix is intact; the name now survives long enough to key the record.

WHY THIS SHAPE
    - No new proxy slots are invented. ProxyManifest.FREE_COUNT is already 50, and slot
      allocation is the existing getUsingBPidL().
    - The guest's process name is not rewritten. Chrome logs and expects
      "com.android.chrome:sandboxed_process0"; changing that would be visible to the app.
    - The 5-argument overload is preserved, so ActivityStack, BJobManagerService, the
      provider path and startService are byte-for-byte unaffected.

SECURITY IMPACT
    None identified. No uid, package name, signature, permission or caller identity is
    altered; the host AMS still receives instanceName = null and still applies its own
    checks to the proxy component. The change is internal bookkeeping about which container
    process a guest's own service instance belongs to.

REGRESSION RISK
    Moderate, and concentrated in one place: every guest process now goes through the
    keyed lookup. For anything that does not use isolated service instances the key is the
    process name, so the behaviour is identical by construction. What genuinely changes is
    that a multi-instance app consumes more of the 50 proxy slots than before -- Chrome may
    take several. Slot exhaustion was already possible and already throws
    "No processes available"; this makes it reachable sooner for renderer-heavy apps.

VERIFICATION BAR (docs/multiapp-vs-duplika-chrome-comparison.md section 10)
    Chrome alive >= 60 s, renderer process created and staying up, New Tab page renders
    real content, relaunch works. Plus the ladder guards -- Level 6, Level 7 split fixture,
    VLC -- because this touches process allocation for every guest, not just Chrome.

